/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5489:- **wish:** …and that is the shape of a check Guardian could own. When a `@embedFile`'d asset changes, the tests that assert on its CONTENT are exactly the ones at risk, and they are mechanically identifiable: `std.mem.indexOf(u8, <name-bound-to-that-embed>, "literal")`. I wrote a throwaway Python sweep to find the rest and it produced ~55 false positives, because in this repo several files bind the identifier `js` to a DIFFERENT embed in each test scope, and a line-based scan cannot tell them apart — Guardian already has the AST index that could. Something like `guardian-check explain asset-assertions --file src/serve/assets/pcb_board.js` listing every test literal asserted against that asset, with present/absent status, would turn a gate cycle (~10 min) into one command.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5490:- **friction:** `file-size` on `src/serve/pcb_layout_page.zig` again (frozen at 10512) — third or fourth entry on this exact item. This task only needed **+1 line** there (a third Appearance tab), but I could not tell whether that was affordable, because the finding's number and the file's actual line count disagree wildly: `wc -l` says 13485, the baseline says 10512. I had to write a Python script that strips `test "…" { … }` blocks to reverse-engineer that Guardian counts non-test lines (10510 — two lines of headroom), and only then knew I had to offset my +1 by shrinking a neighbouring writer. The metric's DEFINITION is the missing piece, not just the headroom: `explain file-size` describes the check but never says what it counts, so an agent budgeting against it is guessing. Printing "src/serve/pcb_layout_page.zig: 10510/10512 non-test lines" on a passing run would have saved the whole detour.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5496:## 2026-08-13 · claude · eda — one PCB colour theme, consumed by every renderer (Phase 3)
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5506:## 2026-08-13 · claude · eda — turn on the relational checks that needed no Guardian code (Wave A)
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5513:- **good:** the whole point of the session worked — two relational rules that needed no Guardian code now hold ratchets on real architecture: `src/kicad_pcb/*` must not import `serve/` (3 sites frozen) and `src/placement/*` must not import the export layer (63 sites across 49 files frozen), plus five `[[ban]]` rules starting at zero debt. `forbidden` matching SUBSTRINGS of the RESOLVED import path is the right call — `../serve/sync.zig` normalizing to `src/serve/sync.zig` before comparison is what let one 8-character rule name the edge.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5517:## 2026-08-13 · claude · eda — two MCP schema↔handler contract fixes (undeclared args + array-vs-comma-string types)
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5521:- **friction:** `failed command: cd . && ./.zig-cache/o/<hash>/test … --listen=-` on a GREEN run, again (eleventh-plus report — logging it as a vote). Cost this session: one round trip. My filtered run printed `guardian/test: 20 test(s) selected by filter … test wall 0.01s` followed immediately by `failed command:`, with no `error:` anywhere, so I ran the cached test binary by hand to get `All 20 tests passed` before I trusted it. Every later invocation was wrapped as `… 2>&1 | tail; echo EXIT=${PIPESTATUS[0]}`. Note the banner also appears when the build genuinely fails for an unrelated reason (a blocking Guardian check), so it is not even a reliable "ignore me" marker — that ambiguity is the expensive part.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5522:- **wish:** a way to assert "this checked list is exhaustive". Both defects here were one side of a contract drifting from the other (an MCP tool's JSON Schema vs. the handler that reads the arguments), and my fix is a table of the three tools that route through a shared options builder, guarded by a comment saying "adding a caller means adding a row here". Guardian already knows how to find call sites — `dead-pub` proves it can see who references a symbol — so a rule like "every caller of `mcp_read_opts.describePcbOpts` must appear in `schema_readers`" would turn my honour-system comment into a real ratchet. Same shape as `fuzz_presence`'s "these modules must keep calling `std.testing.fuzz`", pointed at callers instead of modules.
