/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5527:- **bug:** none found in Guardian; the new `add_zone` spec tag initially reported as unlinked until its matching `SPEC.md` bullet was added.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5531:## 2026-08-13 · claude · eda — export a footprint pad's own rotation to KiCad
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5535:- **wish:** a check for "two readers of one file format must agree on a field". This defect was four parsers of the same `(pad … (pos X Y ROT))` grammar where exactly one read the third token, so netlisp routed and DRC'd a rotated pad and then exported axis-aligned copper for it. Nothing in the gate could see that, because each reader is individually well-formed. My guard is a hand-written differential test that parses one fixture with both readers and asserts the field agrees — which works, but is a pattern I had to invent per site. Guardian already ratchets "these modules must keep calling `std.testing.fuzz`" (`fuzz_presence`); a `[[twin_field]]`-style rule naming two functions that must both mention a token, or simply a report of "form `pos` is read in 4 files with differing arity", would have surfaced this class before it shipped.
