/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5562:## 2026-08-13 · Claude (Fable 5) · eda — port padRotOut diagonal-pad landing into import-kicad's emitBoardPad
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5564:- **friction:** one more `failed command: … ./test … --listen=-` banner on a green run (cost: one redundant full-suite gate re-run). New fact for the triage of the entries above: the line is the pinned Zig toolchain's own — `lib/compiler/Maker/Step/Run.zig` records the child argv as the step's failed-command UNCONDITIONALLY before the pass/fail verdict exists (its comment says so) and only clears it on the success path, so any surviving echo is pre-verdict noise, not a verdict. Guardian can't fix Zig, but the wished-for final `guardian/test: PASS (N run, 0 failed)` line from the counting runner would outrank the banner in every transcript.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5567:## 2026-08-13 · codex · eda — integrate Gerber/spec and field-router branches
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5569:## 2026-08-13 · Claude · guardian-zig — Wave C: three new relational checks (divergent-const, twin-referent, duplicate-json-key)
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5571:- **good:** self-hosting caught a real finding in the very first run of a new check. `divergent-const` fired on Guardian itself: `max_file_bytes` was 16 MiB in `cache.zig` and 4 MiB in `source_digest.zig` (with `walk.WalkOpts.max_file_bytes` a third value, 10 MiB). One name, three facts. Renaming two of them to `max_input_bytes` / `max_source_bytes` was the whole fix, and I would not have gone looking.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:5573:- **friction:** `failed command: ./.zig-cache/o/<hash>/test … --listen=-` on a GREEN run — but this session found a REPRODUCIBLE trigger, which the previous nine-plus reports did not name: it appears when `zig build test` is **piped** (`| tail`, `| grep`), and never when its output is redirected to a file or run bare. `mutation.runner.test.superviseArgv kills the whole process group on timeout` does exactly that — `kill(-pgid)` — and under a pipeline the test binary shares its process group with `tail`, so the signal reaches the pipeline and the build system reports the step as failed while `zig build` still exits 0. Cost here: three extra suite runs before I stopped believing the banner. Suggested fix in Guardian's own suite: have `superviseArgv`'s test put its child in a NEW process group (`setpgid`) before killing, so the blast radius is the child, not whatever shell plumbing the developer used.
