/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:11127:- **good:** self-hosting paid for itself again. Turning off name-based pairing surfaced eight pairs on Guardian's own tree, and three were real duplication rather than protocol shape: `check.splitCsv` was a hand copy of `snapshot_helper.splitNames` (the alias resolution would have drifted between `--only` and `GUARDIAN_UPDATE_SNAPSHOT`), `divergent-const` and `shadowed-const` each had their own copy of the file-scope-const collector at 93% similarity, and `FakeEnv.set` / `FakeFs.writeFile` held two copies of the same three-branch owned-map insert. None of the three shared a function name, so v1 could not have seen any of them.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:11128:- **friction:** adding a five-line `// twin-drift-ok:` annotation above a function in `bool_ops_per_condition.zig` pushed the file from exactly 200 to 205 lines and red-lit `module-doc-header`, whose threshold is 200. The exemption a check asks you to write can therefore trip a different check; I moved the annotation to the other side of the pair. A comment block that exists to satisfy Guardian arguably should not count toward a Guardian line threshold.
