/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:12903:## 2026-09-20 · Claude · mylang — agent-usefulness plan: D1/D2/S1/L1/M1 plus the reference command
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:12910:- **context:** Because mylang's test steps depend on the gated `install mylang`, one missing `spec` bullet anywhere blocks `zig build test-units` for the whole tree — every package's tests, not just the failing one. That is a property of the build graph rather than of Guardian, but it makes the `spec` check the highest-stakes one in the set, and it is why the registration workflow matters more here than the finding count suggests.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:12912:## 2026-09-20 · Claude · mylang — agent-usefulness round 2: C1, I1, the aggregate-argument ABI fix
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:12921:  `src/test_runner.zig` already documents this exact hazard ("Zig's build runner writes a run step's `failed command: …` banner before any verdict exists and erases it only on the success path… Guardian cannot unprint another program's line; it can be the last word", plus the note at line ~602 about a stale banner on piped focused runs), and the 2026-09-19 codex entry reports it too — so the verdict-is-last mitigation is not holding in this shape. Cost here: because three subagents were about to iterate with this command, the lead had to pre-emptively warn each of them not to trust it, and one still asked. If the banner cannot be suppressed, printing the verdict *and* exit status one final time after the build system's own summary — or documenting in the check list that a trailing `failed command:` is expected on focused runs — would end the recurring misread.
/home/epentland/ai/canopy/guardian-zig/FEEDBACK.md:12922:- **friction:** While a change still carries any Guardian finding there is no supported way to build the compiler, and the failure is silent rather than loud. Because `addAllChecks(…, b.getInstallStep(), …)` gates the install, `zig build` compiles the new compiler correctly and then declines to publish it, so `zig-out/bin/mylang` keeps its previous contents and every end-to-end check quietly exercises **old** code. Guardian's own caution line shows the hazard is known ("zig-out binaries predate this failed run — rebuild green before running them"), but the remedy is left to the user; the only workaround found was a helper that reaches into `.zig-cache/o/*/` for the newest artifact, which is a build-internal path and had to be explained to every agent. A supported non-gating artifact step (or a `--stage-artifacts-only` switch) would remove the workaround and the class of stale-binary mistake with it.
